title: Planner statistics
tags: [[Query engine]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
status: proposed
summary: Exact per-field histograms kept as a derived key space, and a cost model that tells a scattered read from a contiguous one, so that the planner chooses from what an operand will read rather than from the query's shape.

The [[Query planner]] was built without statistics, on the belief that the cost
choices left were minor. The cold measurements of November 2026 showed otherwise
(user decision, 9 November 2026: build the statistics). This note is the design.
''Built so far: the histogram, its estimate, probe or walk and the order of an `and`
from them'' (steps 1 to 3, November 2026); the "measured and not built" re-measured
cold (step 4: both hold, [[Query planner]]); step 5 is not done.

!! What showed it

[[The timed regression suite]]'s `--cold` pass (`cold.<scenario>`, the store evicted
from the page cache before each run) on the `L` size, 100 000 files:

|!Scenario |!Cold |
|`query.size_range` — `mfr_size > 900000` over the repository |80–92 ms |
|`query.size_range_in_folder` — the same range below `/dir0/dir1` |''1 270–1 390 ms'' |
|`query.subtree_range_text` — a subtree, `rating > 4` and a name pattern |''1 270–1 320 ms'' |
|`query.finder` |390–420 ms |
|`query.page`, `query.folder` — the cold floor of a first page |≈ 60 ms |

Bounding a range by a folder made it fifteen times ''slower''. Profiled
(`POST /repos/:repo/query/profile` on `L`):

|!Query |!Keys read |!Warm |!Cold |
|`mfr_size > 900000` |11 785 |1 ms |70 ms |
|`/dir0/dir1` (18 983 files) and `mfr_size > 900000` |''38 815'' |27 ms |''1 514 ms'' |

''Probe or walk'' (`index/plan.rs`, `probes`) probed: 18 983 candidates × `PROBE_COST`
(4) = 75 932 < 100 000 holders of `mfr_size`. The rule prices the walk at the field's
''holders'', which is the cost of a `!=` or a pattern — but a range walk reads only
the rows ''inside the range'', here 11 785. So the choice was wrong even counted in
keys, three times; and cold it was wrong by twenty, because a probe's keys are
scattered over the file (a candidate's rows, then the next one's) where a walk's are
contiguous (about a hundred per page). `query.subtree_range_text` is presumed to be
the same case (not yet profiled).

!! Why statistics rather than a bounded walk

The narrow repair is adaptive: walk the range, and switch to probing once the walk has
read `candidates × PROBE_COST` keys — never worse than twice the better side, no state
kept. It mends this one choice, after paying for part of the wrong side. Statistics
inform every choice the planner makes or could make:

* ''probe or walk'', decided before reading;
* ''the order of an `and`'' — by shape only before step 3 (bitmaps first, scans last, in the query's order); with cardinalities, the most selective operand first, each handed the intersection so far;
* ''fetch or walk'' for a sorted page ([[Sorting and postings]]), which already estimates, from little.

Choosing differently never answers differently: an `and` commutes, and a probe and a
walk decide each row by the same key (`tests/index_oracle.rs`). So the plan may depend on
the data without breaking a paginated session — the reason the order was kept a
property of the shape ([[How queries are evaluated]]: the cursor is tied to the
query sent; [[Query planner]]). That property must be restated, not lost: every page
gets the same ''results''; it may get them by another path if the data changed between
pages.

!! The statistics

''Exact, maintained like an index'' — a derived key space ([[Store tables]]), written
in each write's own transaction by `KvTxn::derive_row`, rebuilt by `reindex`, compared
by `check` — rather than sampled and refreshed, which would drift and make plans
depend on when the sample was taken.

* ''A histogram per ordered field'': a count of rows per ''bucket'', a bucket being a prefix of the value key (`kvstore/derived.rs`, `value_key`, `hist_bucket`), so that buckets are contiguous ranges of the partition and a range's estimate is the sum of the buckets it covers, exact but for the one the operand falls in. The prefix width is per type, as each key encodes:
** a number is an order-preserving `f64` (`num_key`): its first 16 bits are the sign, the exponent and 4 bits of mantissa — buckets of 1/16 of an octave, log-scaled, which suits sizes and counts; `int` and `float` share the type tag, so one histogram;
** a date is milliseconds in an `i64` (`dt_key`): the first 16 bits are 9 000 years wide, so a date bucket keeps the first 34 bits — buckets of 2^30 ms, ≈ 12 days —, the rest of the fifth byte cleared;
** a text, its first two bytes; a tree name, likewise after the parent — ''not kept'' (Open below): an estimate for a text operand answers that it does not know.
* ''Set sizes'' are mostly known exactly already: a field's holders (`present`), a posting, a subtree's descendants, a candidate set. They need no new key space, only to be read where the plan is made.

Only the populated buckets are stored, in the table `hist` ([[Store tables]]): a key
`field · bucket` → count of rows (a record holding a value twice counts twice). A row
written or removed changes one count: one key put more per row change of a number or a
date (`KvTxn::derive_row`). `mf repo check` compares each count to what the rows derive
(`check_histograms`); `DERIVED_VERSION` 6 brought the table, so an older store is
reindexed when it opens.

''The estimate'', `KvSource::range_rows(field, op, value)`: the rows the comparison
holds, as `at_least` (the buckets wholly inside) and `at_most`
(those and the operand's bucket). It reads the buckets on the comparison's side of the
operand — one key past every value —, not the rows. A field has one type
([[One value type per field name]]) and an operand of another is refused
([[Comparisons]]), so the histogram of the operand's type is the field's. Tested against a count of the rows
themselves, after writes, edits, deletions and a reindex (`tests/kv_histogram.rs`).

!! The cost model

A cardinality is not a cost: the 38 815 keys above took 27 ms warm and 1.5 s cold. The
model prices a plan in ''contiguous'' and ''scattered'' key reads:

* a walk of a range: its estimated rows, contiguous;
* a probe: per candidate, a scattered read (its uuid, then its rows of the field) — the candidates are in dense-id order, so after a forest-order reindex the rows of one folder's records are close ([[Checking and reindexing a repository]]), which a weight may later reflect;
* a bitmap: a key per 65 536 ids.

The weight of a scattered read against a contiguous one is calibrated with `--cold`,
not guessed; the warm weight is close to 1. One weight is used for both — the plan
should be good cold, where the time is; a weight that makes it worse warm is to be
measured, not assumed.

''Probe or walk'', as built (`KvSource::probes`, `index/plan.rs`): a probe costs
`PROBE_KEYS` (4) keys per candidate, each weighed `SCATTERED` (4) contiguous keys; the
walk of an ordered comparison of a number or a date costs the range's `at_most` rows,
less the rows of the values in the range that have a posting, plus two keys each — a
posted value is walked as its posting (`range_walk`, read from the postings' own keys).
Without the correction, a range over a field of few values (a rating) was priced at its
rows and probed where its walk reads five postings. A `!=` and a text predicate keep the
field's holders. The histogram is read only once the holders leave the choice open. The
weight 4 is a first value from the profile above (≈ 13 walked keys per probed candidate
cold, ≈ 16 warm), not a calibration: the `--cold` run settles it. Tested by
`tests/kv_cost.rs`, `a_narrow_range_in_a_wide_folder_walks_the_range` (a fifth of the
repository in the folder, 1 % in the range: 6 440 keys before, now no more than the two
operands alone).

''Profiled after step 2'' (cold by hand on `L`, the machine of the table above, three
runs each, keys from `query/profile`):

|!Query |!Keys |!Cold |
|`query.size_range_in_folder` |11 760 |87–111 ms (was 1 270–1 390) |
|`query.size_range` |11 786 |84–113 ms |
|`query.subtree_range_text` |3 044 |310–378 ms (was 1 270–1 320) |
|  — the subtree and `rating > 4` alone |2 064 |56–59 ms |
|  — the subtree and the name pattern alone |5 868 |397–456 ms |
|`query.finder` |5 045, 4 805 of them in `prepare` |441–661 ms |
|  — its `osm` on the path alone |5 037 |472–639 ms |

The range in a folder now costs the range. What is left of `subtree_range_text` is
its text leaf on the forest's names, checked on the page ([[Query planner]], the leaves
that wait), and the finder's is the `osm` path's resolution in the forest before the
index runs (`forest_query`) — neither an `and`'s order: its operands are already
evaluated subtree first, then the range, the text last.

''The order of an `and`'', as built (`plan::and_order`, `Eval::scan_estimate`): the
operands answered from a bitmap or a posting first, in the query's order, then the
scans, the narrowest first — an ordered comparison of a number or a date by its
histogram's `at_most` (`Source::range_estimate`), a `!=`, a pattern or a text range by
its field's holders —, ties in the query's order. The estimates are read only when two
scans are to be ordered. Of a pattern every file holds and a range holding 1 % of them,
the query read 32 327 keys pattern first and 644 range first; it reads 644 in either
order (`tests/kv_cost.rs`, `of_two_scans_the_narrower_goes_first`).

The step said "scans still last unless a scan's estimate is the smallest". A scan is not
put before the bitmaps even then: a bitmap is read whole today, handed candidates or not,
and a scan handed them reads no more than its own walk (probe or walk chooses the
cheaper), so evaluating it first could only cost more. That holds of how a bitmap is
read, not of what it could be (Open below). What is left open: an `or`, an `and` or an
`in` nested in the `and` hands its candidates down to its own scans, yet is ordered as a
bitmap, first.

!! Steps

Each one ends with a commit, cost assertions by key count (`tests/kv_cost.rs`,
`KvStore::reads()`, never a duration — doc "Tests that fail under load") and a `--cold`
run on a neutral machine (the user's).

# ''The histogram'' as a derived key space: maintained in `derive_row`, rebuilt by `reindex`, compared by `check_derived`, `DERIVED_VERSION` bumped (a store of the previous format is reindexed when it opens — its migration, doc "Persisted format changes"). An estimate function `range_rows(field, op, value)`, tested against an exact count on the oracle's data. Measure `metarecord.write` and `tree.write` before and after.
# ''Probe or walk'' from the estimate and the cost model, replacing `PROBE_COST` against the holders for the ordered comparisons (`kvstore/source.rs`, `compare`; the text scan at `source.rs:740` keeps the holders, which a pattern does read). Test: the case above reads no more than the walk (`/dir0/dir1` and `mfr_size > 900000` on a generated repository: ≤ the range's keys plus the subtree's).
# ''The order of an `and''' by estimated selectivity, scans still last unless a scan's estimate is the smallest. Profile `query.subtree_range_text` and `query.finder` cold first, to know what they read. Profiled (above): neither is slowed by the order.
# ''Re-measure what was "measured and not built"'' in [[Query planner]]: those conclusions were drawn warm, pricing a random read at ≈ 2 µs; cold, it is about a hundred times that. Done 9 November 2026: ≈ 120 µs a scattered read cold, and both conclusions hold.
# Update [[Query planner]] (statistics, the model, the restated determinism) and drop its "no statistics" — or merge this note into it.

!! Open

* Whether a text histogram is worth its keys (a text range is rarer than a numeric one), and the names of a forest likewise. Not kept for now: a string row costs no more to write than before. The date width was settled at 34 bits without a measurement of what a date range typically spans.
* Whether a bitmap handed few candidates should read only its chunks holding them (a set is a key per 65 536 dense ids, a chunk up to 8 KB) — never more keys than reading it whole, in the same key order. Then a scan of very few rows would be worth evaluating before the bitmaps. Nothing to gain at the sizes measured (100 000 records: two chunks a set); 16 chunks at a million, about 150 at ten. Not built (user, 9 November 2026).
* Whether the name search of a finder can be made local: cold, an `osm` path on `L` costs ≈ 0.4–0.7 s, the rows of ≈ 1 100 nodes each a scattered read (≈ 120 µs). Neither the order of a plan nor another formulation reads fewer of them ([[Query planner]], measured and not built).
* Whether the probe weight should depend on how scattered the candidates are (one folder after a forest-order reindex, against ids spread over the repository).

!! How the measurements were taken

* `scripts/dev/bench.sh --cold --big` (`--filter query.` to narrow); the generated `L` lives in `target/bench-data/L`.
* Keys per phase: a daemon of its own on another port, `./target/release/metafolder-daemon --port 7631 --config crates/daemon/default-config/config.toml`, the requests authorised by `Authorization: Bearer $(cat $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/metafolder/daemon.token)`, then `POST /repos/load` and `POST /repos/:repo/query/profile` with the query's body. Cold by hand: unload, `fsync` and `posix_fadvise(DONTNEED)` on `.metafolder/internal/kv/data.mdb`, load, wait for `GET /repos/:repo` to say `ready`.
* Stop that daemon by its pid, never with `pkill -f` on a pattern the stopping command itself contains.
